  <p class="eyebrow">Clackpad · Terms of service</p>
  <h1>Terms for using Clackpad.</h1>
  <p class="meta-line">Last updated November 2026. Applies to the Clackpad keyboard for Android and its companion screens (Config, Dictionary &amp; Thesaurus, Analytics &amp; Trends, Notes, Extensions, Numpad).</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Clackpad is a keyboard. It runs on your phone, and what you type stays there. There is no account and no server of ours in the loop.</li>
    <li>You may install and use it on your own devices. You may not pass off our name or marks as yours, or use the app to harm other people or their devices.</li>
    <li>The optional features that talk to a service you choose, or to extensions you install, are yours to switch on, and then those services' rules apply to what you send them.</li>
    <li>Clackpad is beta software provided as it is. We do our best, and we do not promise it will never lose a word or crash.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These terms are written in plain language on purpose. If something here is unclear, <a class="inline-link" href="/contact">ask us</a>. How data is handled is covered by the <a class="inline-link" href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>, which is part of these terms.</p>

  <h2>1. Who we are, and what you are agreeing to</h2>
  <p>Clackpad is made by asystemofcells ("we", "us"). By installing or using Clackpad you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, do not use it, and uninstall it. If you use Clackpad on behalf of an organisation, you confirm you may accept these terms for it.</p>

  <h2>2. What you may do</h2>
  <p>We give you a personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable permission to install Clackpad on devices you own or control, and to use it for typing, notes, dictionary lookups and the other features it ships with. The source code, where it is published, is covered by the licence file in its repository, which is separate from these terms and prevails for anything the licence covers.</p>
  <p>The name Clackpad, the asystemofcells name and mark, the keycap art, the fonts and the other designs in the app belong to us or to their respective authors (see the open-source notices screen in the app for the third-party parts and their licences). Using the app does not give you a right to use those names or marks for anything else.</p>

  <h2>3. What you must not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use Clackpad to break the law, to harass or deceive others, or to attack another service or device.</li>
    <li>Reverse-engineer or repackage the app and distribute the result under our name or in a way that suggests we made or endorse it.</li>
    <li>Remove or hide required notices, or the watermark in the free version, other than by buying Clackpad Pro.</li>
    <li>Publish an extension that hides what it does, asks for more than it needs, or tries to read what people type without saying so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Your typing, and your data</h2>
  <p>What you type is processed on your phone and handed only to the app you are typing into. Clackpad does not collect or transmit it. The exceptions are the optional features in section 5, which you switch on yourself. Nothing is read from password fields, and incognito and never-learn fields are respected. The <a class="inline-link" href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> and the in-app Privacy screen give the details, and they are the authority on data.</p>
  <p>You are responsible for your own content, and for keeping a copy of anything that matters. Clackpad includes features that hold text briefly (clipboard history, text recovery, notes), and none of them is a backup service. Android's own backup may copy some of your settings and notes to your own Google account, under Google's terms.</p>

  <h2>5. Optional features that use something outside the app</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bring your own AI.</strong> If you enter your own endpoint and key, the text you choose to rewrite, complete or translate is sent to that endpoint when you tap the action. That provider's terms, fees and privacy practices apply, and you are responsible for the key and any charges on it. Do not send text you are not permitted to share.</li>
    <li><strong>On-device AI.</strong> Where your phone supports it, Clackpad can use the AI models built into Android or a model you download. Their output can be wrong, so read it before you send it.</li>
    <li><strong>Online GIF search.</strong> If you add your own Tenor key, your search words go to Tenor, under its terms.</li>
    <li><strong>Voice dictation</strong> uses your phone's speech recogniser, asked for the microphone only when you tap it.</li>
    <li><strong>Shizuku and display-over-other-apps</strong> are optional Android permissions that you grant or withhold in system settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Extensions</h2>
  <p>Extensions are optional add-ons, made by us or by others. The catalog lists their permissions, and an extension installs switched off. We review what we list, but a review is not a guarantee: you install an extension at your own risk, and its author, not us, is responsible for it unless it says it is ours. You can remove one at any time, or turn them all off with safe mode. We may remove an extension from the catalog, and stop offering updates for it, if it breaks these terms or puts people at risk.</p>

  <h2>7. Clackpad Pro and payments</h2>
  <p>Clackpad Pro is an optional one-time unlock that removes the watermark and adds the extra customisation listed on its page. Typing, gestures, the trackpad and glide typing stay free. Purchases are made through Google Play, which takes the payment, keeps your purchase record and handles refunds under Google Play's own terms and policies. We never see your payment details. The tip jar, if you use it, is a voluntary gift, not a purchase, and gives you nothing in return beyond our thanks.</p>

  <h2>8. Beta software, and updates</h2>
  <p>Clackpad is in beta. Features may change, move or be withdrawn, and we may release updates, including ones that change how a feature works. We try to keep your settings and layouts intact across updates, but we cannot promise it. A keyboard sits in the path of everything you type, so if it ever misbehaves you can switch to another keyboard from Android's input settings, and uninstalling removes all of Clackpad's data from your phone.</p>

  <h2>9. No warranty</h2>
  <p>To the extent the law allows, Clackpad is provided "as is" and "as available", without promises that it will be error-free, uninterrupted, suitable for a particular purpose or compatible with every app and device. Prediction, autocorrect and AI features make mistakes, so check anything important before you send it. Nothing in these terms limits any rights you have under the consumer law where you live that cannot be limited by agreement.</p>

  <h2>10. Limit of liability</h2>
  <p>To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, lost data, lost profit or business interruption arising from your use of Clackpad, and our total liability for any claim relating to the app is limited to the amount you paid us for it (for the free app, nothing). This does not limit liability that the law does not allow us to limit, such as for fraud or for death or personal injury caused by negligence.</p>

  <h2>11. Ending these terms</h2>
  <p>You can stop at any time by uninstalling Clackpad. We may suspend or end your permission to use it if you seriously or repeatedly break these terms. Sections that by their nature should survive (such as the disclaimers, the limit of liability and the notices on names and marks) do.</p>

  <h2>12. Changes</h2>
  <p>If we change these terms, the new version will be posted here with a new "last updated" date, and for a material change the app's About screen will say so. Using Clackpad after a change means you accept it. If you do not, stop using it.</p>

  <h2>13. Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, complaints, or a report that an extension or the app is misbehaving: use the <a class="inline-link" href="/contact">contact page</a>, or write to <a class="inline-link" href="mailto:clackpad@asystemofcells.com">clackpad@asystemofcells.com</a>.</p>
